---

<BaseLayout
  title="Power Washing Before Painting in Florida | Go Pro Guide"
  description="Why Central Florida exteriors need power washing and dry time before paint. Sun, mildew, stucco prep, and adhesion explained by a local contractor."
  ogImage="/images/power-washing.jpg"
  schema={{
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@graph": [
      {
        "@type": "Article",
        "@id": "https://goprohomeimprovements.com/guides/power-washing-before-painting-florida/#article",
        "headline": "Power Washing Before Painting in Florida",
        "description": "Why Central Florida exteriors need thorough power washing and dry time before painting.",
        "author": {
          "@type": "Organization",
          "name": "GO PRO HOME IMPROVEMENTS INC."
        },
        "publisher": {
          "@type": "Organization",
          "name": "GO PRO HOME IMPROVEMENTS INC.",
          "logo": {
            "@type": "ImageObject",
            "url": "https://goprohomeimprovements.com/images/logo.png"
          }
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
        "@id": "https://goprohomeimprovements.com/guides/power-washing-before-painting-florida/#breadcrumbs",
        "itemListElement": [
          {
            "@type": "ListItem",
            "position": 1,
            "name": "Home",
            "item": "https://goprohomeimprovements.com"
          },
          {
            "@type": "ListItem",
            "position": 2,
            "name": "Guides",
            "item": "https://goprohomeimprovements.com/guides"
          },
          {
            "@type": "ListItem",
            "position": 3,
            "name": "Power Washing Before Painting",
            "item": "https://goprohomeimprovements.com/guides/power-washing-before-painting-florida"
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }}
>
  <Header />

  <section class="bg-gopro-charcoal text-white py-16 border-b-4 border-gopro-orange">
    <div class="container-custom">
      <nav class="text-sm mb-6 font-sans">
        <a href="/" class="text-gray-400 hover:text-gopro-orange transition-colors">Home</a>
        <span class="mx-2 text-gray-500">/</span>
        <a href="/guides" class="text-gray-400 hover:text-gopro-orange transition-colors">Guides</a>
        <span class="mx-2 text-gray-500">/</span>
        <span class="text-white">Power Washing Before Painting</span>
      </nav>
      <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-4 tracking-tight">Power Washing Before Painting in Florida</h1>
      <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 max-w-3xl font-sans">
        Why Central Florida exteriors need a thorough wash and dry time before paint. What happens if you skip it.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-padding">
    <div class="container-custom">
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto">
        <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none">
          <p class="text-xl text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-6 font-sans">
            You can't paint a dirty wall. That's the short version. The long version is that Central Florida exteriors collect mildew, algae, dirt, pollen, and loose paint faster than cooler climates, and skipping the <a href="/services/power-washing" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">power wash</a> before <a href="/services/interior-exterior-painting" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">painting</a> guarantees peeling within a year or two.
          </p>

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-6 tracking-tight mt-12">Why Power Washing Matters</h2>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Paint bonds to the surface underneath. If that surface is coated in green algae, black mildew, or a layer of dust and grime, the paint bonds to the grime—not the siding or stucco. When the grime eventually loosens (and it will), the paint comes with it.
          </p>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Florida's humidity and year-round warmth create perfect conditions for mildew and algae. North-facing walls that never get direct sun stay damp longer and turn green faster. South and west walls bake in UV, which breaks down old paint and leaves a chalky residue. Either way, a clean surface is step one of any lasting paint job.
          </p>

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-6 tracking-tight mt-12">The Dry Time Problem</h2>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            After power washing, the surface needs to dry completely—not just surface-dry, but fully cured so no trapped moisture sits under the paint. In Central Florida's humidity, that takes longer than you'd think.
          </p>
          <div class="bg-gopro-orange/10 border-l-4 border-gopro-orange p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-gray-700 font-sans mb-0">
              <strong class="font-bold">Rule of thumb:</strong> Wait 48-72 hours after power washing before priming or painting. If it rains or the humidity stays above 80%, wait another day. Painting over trapped moisture causes bubbling, poor adhesion, and mildew growth under the paint film.
            </p>
          </div>

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-6 tracking-tight mt-12">Stucco & Masonry Need Extra Time</h2>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Stucco, concrete block, and cinder block are porous. They absorb water during the power wash, and that water takes days to evaporate out. Wood and vinyl siding dry faster. If you're painting stucco in summer humidity, give it three full days minimum after washing. If you're painting in <a href="/areas/kissimmee" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">Kissimmee</a> or <a href="/areas/orlando" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">Orlando</a> during rainy season, wait longer or schedule around the forecast.
          </p>

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-6 tracking-tight mt-12">What About Pressure?</h2>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Too much pressure damages siding, forces water behind trim, and strips paint that should stay. Not enough pressure leaves dirt and mildew. We adjust pressure by surface type:
          </p>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-6 space-y-2 text-gray-700 font-sans">
            <li><strong>Vinyl siding:</strong> Soft wash (low pressure, cleaning solution). High pressure can crack vinyl or force water behind panels.</li>
            <li><strong>Wood siding:</strong> Medium pressure. High pressure gouges soft wood.</li>
            <li><strong>Stucco:</strong> Medium pressure. The texture hides dirt, so you need enough force to clean deep without blasting the surface.</li>
            <li><strong>Concrete & block:</strong> Higher pressure. Durable surface handles it, and you need the power to remove embedded grime.</li>
          </ul>

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-6 tracking-tight mt-12">What Happens If You Skip the Wash?</h2>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Paint peels early. Mildew grows through the paint. The finish looks good for six months, then starts failing. You end up repainting sooner, which costs more than doing it right the first time. Every exterior paint job we do in <a href="/areas/celebration" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">Celebration</a>, <a href="/areas/st-cloud" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">St. Cloud</a>, or <a href="/areas/poinciana" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">Poinciana</a> starts with a wash. No exceptions.
          </p>

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-6 tracking-tight mt-12">Common Questions</h2>
          
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-white border-l-4 border-gopro-orange p-6">
              <h3 class="text-xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-2 tracking-wide">Can I just hose it down instead of power washing?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 font-sans mb-0">
                A garden hose doesn't have enough pressure to remove mildew, algae, or chalky paint residue. You'll wet the dirt, but you won't clean it. Power washing is the only way to prep properly.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-white border-l-4 border-gopro-orange p-6">
              <h3 class="text-xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-2 tracking-wide">Do I need to power wash if the house looks clean?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 font-sans mb-0">
                Yes. Mildew and algae grow in the pores of siding even when the surface looks clean. You can't see it, but it's there, and it prevents paint adhesion. A quick wash solves it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-white border-l-4 border-gopro-orange p-6">
              <h3 class="text-xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-2 tracking-wide">Can I paint the same day if it's sunny?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 font-sans mb-0">
                No. Even in direct sun, trapped moisture takes 48+ hours to escape porous surfaces. Surface-dry doesn't mean fully dry. Wait at least two days.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-6 tracking-tight mt-12">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Power washing before painting isn't optional in Florida. It's the foundation of a paint job that lasts. Clean surface + proper dry time = good adhesion. Skip either one, and you're repainting in two years instead of seven.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-lg p-8 my-8">
            <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide">Need Your Exterior Painted?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 font-sans mb-6">
              We power wash, wait for full dry time, prime, and paint with Florida-rated exterior coatings. Serving <a href="/areas/kissimmee" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">Kissimmee</a>, <a href="/areas/orlando" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">Orlando</a>, and Central Florida since 2014.
            </p>
            <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4">
              <a href="/estimate" class="btn-primary">
                Get Free Estimate
              </a>
              <a href="tel:4072446873" class="btn-secondary">
                Call (407) 244-6873
              </a>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="mt-12 pt-8 border-t border-gray-200">
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 font-sans">
              <a href="/guides" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">← Back to All Guides</a>
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
